WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.580
What do big O, big

2
00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:03.880
Omega, and big Theta actually

3
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:05.720
mean? We will build their

4
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.040
definitions around one running-cost function,

5
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.980
prove its bounds, and finish

6
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.120
with the mistakes that make

7
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:12.940
an otherwise sensible answer wrong.

8
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.440
First say what n measures

9
00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.280
and what operation you count.

10
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.540
For a sorting problem, n

11
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:20.600
might be the number of

12
00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.420
records, while the cost counts

13
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.180
key comparisons; copies and swaps

14
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.400
would be different counters.

15
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.700
Suppose our chosen cost is

16
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:29.980
three n squared plus seven

17
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.120
n plus four. The whole

18
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.160
expression matters for exact work,

19
00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:35.940
but its quadratic term will

20
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.600
control its asymptotic growth.

21
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.040
Big O gives an eventual

22
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.920
upper bound, allowing a positive

23
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.700
constant multiplier. There must be

24
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.620
one constant c and one

25
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.380
threshold n zero such that

26
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.180
every input size beyond that

27
00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:52.080
threshold satisfies f of n

28
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:53.660
at most c times g

29
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:54.040
of n.

30
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.060
For every n at least

31
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.500
one, seven n is at

32
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.460
most seven n squared, and

33
00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.820
four is at most four

34
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:02.760
n squared. Adding these inequalities

35
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.100
puts our whole function at

36
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.880
most fourteen n squared, so

37
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.580
c equals fourteen is a

38
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:08.860
valid upper-bound witness.

39
00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.700
Big Omega reverses the inequality

40
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.700
and supplies an eventual lower

41
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.880
bound. Here the positive extra

42
00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.560
terms cannot reduce the quadratic

43
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.080
term, so our function is

44
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.460
at least three n squared

45
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.680
for every positive n.

46
00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.500
Big Theta requires both sides

47
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.760
with positive constants. We have

48
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.560
already trapped the function between

49
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.060
three n squared and fourteen

50
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:32.200
n squared, which proves a

51
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.620
tight quadratic bound.

52
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.400
A tight asymptotic bound does

53
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.940
not mean exact equality: the

54
00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.640
function need not equal n

55
00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.540
squared, and the two bounding

56
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.140
constants may differ. Theta groups

57
00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:46.900
functions whose growth differs by

58
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.000
at most constant factors after

59
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:49.920
a suitable threshold.

60
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.900
Our function is also big

61
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.720
O of n cubed, because

62
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.160
n squared is at most

63
00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:57.640
n cubed once n reaches

64
00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.880
one. That upper bound is

65
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.760
true but loose, so use

66
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.100
Theta when you have actually

67
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.500
established a tight order.

68
00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.740
A ratio limit can provide

69
00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.360
a convenient shortcut when it

70
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.760
exists. Dividing our function by

71
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.500
n squared gives three plus

72
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:14.060
seven over n plus four

73
00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:15.840
over n squared, whose limit

74
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.500
is the positive constant three.

75
00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.740
A ratio approaching zero supplies

76
00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.320
an upper bound, while a

77
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.340
ratio growing without limit supplies

78
00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.400
a lower bound. These are

79
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.140
sufficient tests under the usual

80
00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.180
positivity assumptions; an oscillating ratio

81
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.320
can lack a limit even

82
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.160
when asymptotic bounds exist.

83
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.560
Now separate the notation from

84
00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.720
the input case being analysed.

85
00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:39.980
Big O does not mean

86
00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:41.900
worst case, and big Omega

87
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:43.620
does not mean best case;

88
00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.000
either can describe a best-case,

89
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.180
worst-case, or suitably defined expected-cost

90
00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:49.680
function.

91
00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.860
For example, a worst-case function

92
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.320
may have both a quadratic

93
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.500
upper bound and a quadratic

94
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.140
lower bound. An average-case statement

95
00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:01.280
additionally needs a distribution on

96
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.280
inputs, so do not hide

97
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:04.820
that assumption behind a symbol.

98
00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:06.520
Things to take note of:

99
00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.500
name the input size and

100
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.020
counted operation, keep the constants

101
00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.000
fixed, and make the inequality

102
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.100
hold eventually for every size.

103
00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:17.560
Prove both sides for Theta,

104
00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.680
distinguish a tight bound from

105
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.260
a merely valid one, and

106
00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:22.620
stop making the letters do

107
00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:23.540
the job of a case

108
00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.420
definition, hmph.
